00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.175
That's my goal.
It's a great goal.

321
00:11:45.175 --> 00:11:47.850
There's only so much that we can do at this point

322
00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.540
given that this challenge is only three weeks away.

323
00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.800
So it's not like you've got six months

324
00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.020
to prepare for this thing,

325
00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:55.303
you've done what you've done leading up to it,

326
00:11:55.303 --> 00:11:57.810
you've put in some miles.

327
00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.770
So you're comfortable running distances,

328
00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:01.990
but you've never done anything

329
00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.533
that involves sleep deprivation

330
00:12:03.533 --> 00:12:07.150
or anything that involves this number of miles

331
00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:08.770
in such a compressed period of time.

332
00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:11.152
And so when I think about how you can best

333
00:12:11.152 --> 00:12:14.830
prepare yourself for this, there's a couple things.

334
00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.854
First of all, nothing about this involves speed or power.

335
00:12:18.854 --> 00:12:23.750
This is all about resilience and persistent motion.

336
00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:26.470
You don't need to go out and do intervals,

337
00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:30.970
you don't need to run any faster than a conversational pace.

338
00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:33.194
And I would go so far as to suggest that

339
00:12:33.194 --> 00:12:37.150
a great method to start to rehearse

340
00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.210
is a walk-run method going into this.

341
00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.510
And you know, for people that are runners

342
00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.070
talking about walking feels like a weakness

343
00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.830
but when you do it properly,

344
00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:51.030
implementing a strategic walk run strategy

345
00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.570
into your training and your participation in the event,

346
00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:57.910
will actually provide dividends beyond what you can imagine.

347
00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.500
Because if you're training your walk,

348
00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:04.165
you can actually walk faster than a jog, like over time.

349
00:13:04.165 --> 00:13:07.113
And it provides your heart and your lungs the ability

350
00:13:07.113 --> 00:13:11.070
to get these mini recoveries within the runs.

351
00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.704
So I would suggest to you

352
00:13:12.704 --> 00:13:16.294
that the overarching kind of philosophy right now

353
00:13:16.294 --> 00:13:20.370
should be how do I get my body ready to be

354
00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:24.250
in a state of persistent motion when it's very fatigued?

355
00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:27.010
So that means that you don't need to go out

356
00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:29.325
and do 12 or 15 mile runs.

357
00:13:29.325 --> 00:13:33.430
You're better off going out for maybe an eight-mile run

358
00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:36.580
where every, every mile or half mile

359
00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.905
you walk for five minutes.

360
00:13:37.905 --> 00:13:40.260
And the idea is getting through all of these workouts

361
00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.760
feeling fresh and then perhaps having days

362
00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.785
where you're doing short runs,

363
00:13:47.785 --> 00:13:51.330
and I use the term run, you know, very broadly.

364
00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:55.170
Like these can be very easy, runs, light jogs

365
00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.270
but you're doing two or three throughout the day.

366
00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.370
So you're getting, you're acclimating your body

367
00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:01.510
to kind of always being moving.

368
00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.210
And it has nothing to do with being fast

369
00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.970
or being winded or anything like that.

370
00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:07.710
It's really about can you keep moving forward

371
00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:08.558
when you're tired?

372
00:14:08.558 --> 00:14:09.391
Which brings up of course-

373
00:14:09.391 --> 00:14:12.300
How many days a week should I do,

374
00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:13.740
that this weekend, next week-

375
00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.820
Well I think in a broader context.

376
00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.495
I would approach this from a perspective

377
00:14:18.495 --> 00:14:21.340
of creating a periodized training program

378
00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:22.520
where you're building your base,

379
00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:23.640
then you're building strength.

380
00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.390
Then you're, you know, getting a little bit of power,

381
00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:27.740
you're having easy weeks

382
00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.100
and then you're having harder weeks.

383
00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.260
And then you enter into the phase

384
00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:33.755
where you're really getting race specific

385
00:14:33.755 --> 00:14:37.840
and you're doing periodic simulation days

386
00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.850
which I think you can still do and we can talk about

387
00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:44.030
which is where you kind of mimic the actual event.

388
00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.750
Like 42-mile run?

389
00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.674
So like, yeah, like when I was training for Ultraman,

390
00:14:48.674 --> 00:14:53.131
like two months out I picked three days

391
00:14:53.131 --> 00:14:55.760
or my coach, Chris and I picked, you know,

392
00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.752
okay, this week on Friday, Saturday, Sunday,

393
00:14:57.752 --> 00:15:01.144
you're gonna do 70% of the Ultraman event.

394
00:15:01.144 --> 00:15:03.460
And then we're gonna have kind of a rest week.

395
00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:04.966
And then we're gonna build up towards

396
00:15:04.966 --> 00:15:06.350
over the next couple of weeks

397
00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:08.725
to a second race simulation weekend

398
00:15:08.725 --> 00:15:11.930
where we're gonna do 80% of the distance.

399
00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:13.980
We're gonna repeat that, have a rest week,

400
00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:16.943
and then build up again and then do 90% of it.

401
00:15:16.943 --> 00:15:19.360
And that serves many masters.

402
00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:24.404
First of all, it's giving your body a sense

403
00:15:24.404 --> 00:15:28.630
of what this endeavor is gonna look like and feel like.

404
00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:30.166
And also from a mental perspective,

405
00:15:30.166 --> 00:15:34.180
once you complete those simulation weekends

406
00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:35.622
you're like, oh, I can,

407
00:15:35.622 --> 00:15:37.913
now I can wrap my head around this crazy thing

408
00:15:37.913 --> 00:15:39.846
that I never thought I could do before.

409
00:15:39.846 --> 00:15:42.700
In your case, I would say we're three weeks out.

410
00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:47.700
So maybe you can do two or three sim days

411
00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:50.704
between now and the event where you do,

412
00:15:50.704 --> 00:15:54.840
you know, 60% of the four by four by 48

413
00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:59.840
where you're running like two miles every four hours

414
00:16:00.044 --> 00:16:04.060
for, you know, 24 hours or something like that.

415
00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:05.730
And you're like, okay, I did that.

416
00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:08.710
And then maybe, probably just, you are only three weeks out.

417
00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:12.010
So I would suggest maybe two sim days and then a second one

